Still one warning about `row_used` never read. Which assignment? Probably the `row_used = true` in the bare-cell branch at the end of the loop iteration — its value is only read if the NEXT child is a GRID_ROW; if the loop ends, it's never read. But that's a real read pattern. Or maybe the final `row_used = false` after the GRID_ROW is never read if the loop ends. The compiler's "value assigned is never read" is a conservative warning — it flags assignments whose value is guaranteed overwritten before any read in some path.

Let me look at which one. The compiler warns when an assignment is never read. Consider: in the bare-cell branch, `row_used = true` at the end — is it ever read? Only by the GRID_ROW branch's `if row_used`. If the last child is a bare cell, that assignment is never read → warning. And in the GRID_ROW branch, `row_used = false` after — only read by a subsequent GRID_ROW's check; if the last child is a GRID_ROW, never read.

The cleanest fix: avoid the flag entirely. Instead, track whether the current row has content via `col` (col > 0 means the current row has cells) OR whether we've placed anything in the current row. Actually for a GRID_ROW starting a new row: it needs to advance past the previous row IF the previous row has content. "Has content" = col > 0 OR the previous item was a GRID_ROW that placed cells... Hmm.

Alternative clean approach: iterate and track `last_row_end` — the row index where the previous item ended. For a GRID_ROW: start a new row = `row = prev_end_row` (the next row after the previous content). Let me restructure without a flag:

```rust
let mut row = 0usize;
let mut col = 0usize;
for &child in &node.children {
    let Some(cn) = nodes.get(&child) else { continue };
    if cn.component == component_type::GRID_ROW {
        // A GRID_ROW starts a NEW row: advance to the next row after the
        // previous content (a used row always advances; an empty grid starts
        // at row 0).
        row += if col > 0 || row > 0 { 1 } else { 0 };
        // hmm, col>0 means a bare-cell run ended mid-row → new row.
        // row>0 means a GRID_ROW already used a row.
        col = 0;
        for &cell in &cn.children {
            placements.push((cell, row, col));
            col += 1;
        }
        row += 1;
        col = 0;
    } else {
        if col >= columns { row += 1; col = 0; }
        placements.push((child, row, col));
        col += 1;
    }
}
```

Wait — this has a subtle issue. Consider: GRID_ROW(2 cells) then bare cells. After the GRID_ROW, `row += 1; col = 0`. So the next bare cell places at (row, 0). Good. Then GRID_ROW after bare cells: `col > 0` (bare run) → advance. Good. GRID_ROW after GRID_ROW: after the first GRID_ROW, row was advanced and col=0; the second GRID_ROW checks `col > 0 || row > 0` → row>0 → advances again. Good.

But there's an edge: a bare-cell run that exactly fills `columns` → col wraps to 0 and row advances. Then a GRID_ROW: `col > 0` is false, `row > 0` is true → advances again. Good (the GRID_ROW goes to the next row after the filled row).

Another edge: bare-cell run ends exactly at columns boundary (col==0 after wrap) and no GRID_ROW follows — fine.

Edge: first child is a bare cell: col=0,row=0 → places at (0,0), col=1. Good.

Edge: first child is a GRID_ROW: `col > 0 || row > 0` → false → row stays 0. Places cells at row 0. Then row=1. Good.

Edge: bare cells that fill a row exactly (col==columns) then wrap: the wrap advances row and col=0. Then a GRID_ROW: row>0 → advance. Good.

So the flag-free version works. Let me rewrite without `row_used`.

